<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yarkent Khanate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yarkent</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yarkent Khanate was a successor state of the eastern Chagatai realm, centred on the oasis city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkand">Yarkand</a> in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>. The eastern khanate split around 1462 into the Yarkent and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a> khanates. Chagatayid rulers continued to govern from Yarkand for generations, preserving the dynastic line in the region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yarkent-khanate.jpg" alt="Yarkent Khanate" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: naturalearthdata.com, offered to the Public Domain per Terms of Use · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Yarkent_Khanate.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Yarkent Khanate, also called the Yarkand Khanate and the Kashghar Khanate, was a Sunni Muslim khanate ruled by Turkified Mongol descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khan">Chagatai Khan</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>. Its ruling house belonged to the Eastern Chaghatayid, or Moghul, dynasty. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> collapsed in the first half of the 14th century. Its western part became the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> in 1370, and its eastern part became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a>, created by Tughluk Timur Khan in 1347 with its capital at Almalik, in the Ili River Valley. Moghulistan comprised the settled lands of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgaria">Kashgaria</a> and the regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hami">Kumul</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dughlat">Dughlats</a> had ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgaria">Kashgaria</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> since 1220, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khan">Chagatai Khan</a> granted them most of it. In 1509 they rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a>. Sultan Said Khan, a brother of the Khan of Eastern Moghulistan, or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turpan-khanate">Turpan Khanate</a>, then conquered them, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>, Yarkent and Hotan from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirza-abu-bakr-dughlat">Mirza Abu Bakr Dughlat</a> and set up his own khanate. Yarkent was its capital until its fall in 1705, as it had been for the Dughlat state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The khanate was predominantly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> and Turki, and its populous cities included Hotan, Yarkent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>, Yangihissar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksu">Aksu</a>, Kucha, Karashar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hami">Kumul</a>. Its conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dughlat">Dughlats</a> ended their dominance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgaria">Kashgaria</a> and made it the foremost power of the region. Said Khan's reign was heavily influenced by the khojas, and he had a close relationship with his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. He campaigned in Bolor in 1527 to 1528, raided Badakhshan in 1529, and led looting expeditions into Ladakh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> in 1532. He reportedly died in 1533 at Daulat Beg Oldi of high-altitude pulmonary edema while returning from Ladakh and Kashmir.</p><p>His successor Abdurashid Khan, who ruled from 1533 to 1565, began by executing a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dughlat">Dughlat</a> family. He fought the Kirghiz and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> for control of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a>, which was ultimately lost, so that the Moghuls were largely restricted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>. The khanate enjoyed dominance in the region for about 200 years, until the Buddhist Dzungar Khanate conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altishahr">Altishahr</a> from 1678 to 1705, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsewang-rabtan">Tsewang Rabtan</a> took the khanate in 1705.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yarkent Khanate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the late fifteenth century it split between rival khans and gave way to a separate eastern khanate based at Turpan and to the <b>Yarkent Khanate</b> founded in 1514.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Galdan conquered the <b>Yarkent Khanate</b> in 1680, intervening on behalf of the Afaqi khojas, and campaigned against the Kazakhs and the Kyrgyz.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Military</a></li></ul></section>
